#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093189} :activity/id #uuid "f7750f7e-ea53-5456-8042-5c68eaecd261" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050209 :topic/id #uuid "368ceda4-e93c-5300-8a14-cc8ed01f5a93" :topic/math-academy-id 972} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093189 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093470 :tutorial/content "When solving equations containing hyperbolic functions, one strategy that often works is to rewrite any hyperbolic functions using their definitions. Doing this reduces the problem to solving an exponential equation.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following equation:\n\n$$\n2\\cosh{x} = 3\n$$\n\nTo find the real solutions to this equation, we first recall that the definition of $\\cosh{x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\cosh{x} = \\dfrac12(e^x + e^{-x})\n$$\n\nSubstituting this definition into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac12(e^x+e^{-x}) \\right) = 3\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\ne^x + e^{-x} = 3\n$$\n\nWe can eliminate the $e^{-x}$ term by multiplying both sides of the equation by $e^x$ and simplifying by bringing all terms to one side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{x} \\cdot (e^{x} + e^{-x}) &= e^{x} \\cdot 3 \\\\\ne^{x} \\cdot e^{x} + e^{x} \\cdot e^{-x} &= 3e^{x} \\\\\ne^{2x} + 1 &= 3e^{x} \\\\\ne^{2x} - 3e^{x} + 1 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is a quadratic equation in $e^x$. To see this, note that we can write our equation as\n\n$$\n\\left(e^{x}\\right)^2 - 3e^x + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nand if we let $y=e^x$, we get\n\n$$\ny^2 - 3y + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation using the quadratic formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= (- (-3) ± \\sqrt{(-3)^{2} - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1})/(2 \\cdot 1) \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3 ± \\sqrt{9 - 4}}{2} \\\\\ny &= \\frac{3 ± \\sqrt{5}}{2} \\\\\ne^{x} &= \\frac{3 ± \\sqrt{5}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the natural logarithm of both sides, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\ln{\\left(\\dfrac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)}\n$$\n\nSince we want real solutions only, we require that the argument of the logarithm is positive. However, since\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 + \\sqrt{5}}{2} > 0\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\dfrac{3 - \\sqrt{5}}{2} > 0\n$$\n\nthis means that *both* of our solutions are valid.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the solutions to our equation are\n\n$$\nx=\\ln{\\left(\\dfrac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{5}}{2}\\right)}\n$$\n\nWhen solving these equations, we often skip the intermediate step of setting $y=e^x$. However, you can continue to do this if you wish." :tutorial/id #uuid "784cb533-655f-5e6e-bee6-3abed97bd388" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6092 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7c211b14-dc28-5522-a231-ab736cca558a" :step/math-academy-id 37044 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093192}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093190 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092307 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092154 :question/id #uuid "939d725b-cda6-539e-a4a4-ecd426edd6df" :question/math-academy-id "e-9849"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a06fde5-14f9-5285-b92d-f08820423ff8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092549}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Containing a Hyperbolic Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "1c405839-3218-5def-b570-d4ac19be3800" :step/math-academy-id 37045 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093191}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093191 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092308 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092155 :question/id #uuid "7c4bceb1-413e-530e-8caa-beb9490cdb42" :question/math-academy-id "e-9850"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e30c14-d6f7-5585-bad2-4ddff6ed297f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092933}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Containing Multiple Hyperbolic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "7e687e18-8da5-57ef-9742-c99cfda0e04a" :step/math-academy-id 37046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093192 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092309 :know